WR2 and WR9 are chip-wide, and SunOS writes them through channel B

WR2 (the interrupt vector base) and WR9 (Master Interrupt Control) are shared
by both channels of a real Z8530 and may be written through either one.  The
model's channel-B case has the two lines for them commented out, so a
channel-B write to either lands in `default:' -- pointer reset, data silently
dropped.  The comment stating the correct behaviour is still there, directly
above them; only the code implementing it is disabled, and it arrived that way
in the first upload, so there is no recorded reason.

It matters because MIE is the single bit that decides whether the chip can
raise an interrupt at all -- `int_n' is `master_int_enable' ANDed with every
pending source -- and SunOS enables it through channel B.  zsattach()
(sundev/zs_common.c:196-216) walks the two ports and leaves its pointer on
port B, then does

    ZWRITE(9, ZSWR9_MASTER_IE + ZSWR9_VECTOR_INCL_STAT);
    if (vector)
        ZWRITE(2, vector);

so on a Sun-2 the SCC never interrupts, for the whole life of the machine.
The boot PROM does not notice: it polls RR0 and writes the data register and
never touches WR9 (mon/kernel/busyio.c:17-50).  The kernel does not notice
either, because kernel printf goes out through the PROM's putchar vector
(sys/sun/cons.c:332).  Only userspace does -- /dev/console goes through the
interrupt-driven zs driver (sys/sun2/autoconf.c:614-624), where zsstart()
writes the first byte itself and then waits forever for a transmit interrupt.

WR9's reset commands (bits 7:6) already work from either channel, decoded
separately at `wr9_write_evt', which is what makes the chip look healthy:
ZWRITE(9, ZSWR9_RESET_WORLD) at zs_common.c:195 does take effect.

The model's own testbench passes 22 tests over this because every WR9 write in
it targets channel A (z8530_scc_tb.sv lines 316, 980, 1012, 1032, 1101, 1137),
as does every WR2 write (line 1097).  The channel-B path for both is never
exercised.

Both branches are arms of the same always block, so restoring them introduces
no second driver.

--- a/z8530_scc.sv
+++ b/z8530_scc.sv
@@ -1735,13 +1735,13 @@
                     // WR2 and WR9 are chip-wide shared registers on the real
                     // Z8530: writable through either channel. Stored as the
                     // _a copies; channel-B writes land in the same regs.
-                    //@4'd2: begin wr2_a <= data_in; reg_ptr_b <= 4'd0; end
+                    4'd2: begin wr2_a <= data_in; reg_ptr_b <= 4'd0; end
                     4'd3: begin wr3_b <= data_in; reg_ptr_b <= 4'd0; end
                     4'd4: begin wr4_b <= data_in; reg_ptr_b <= 4'd0; end
                     4'd5: begin wr5_b <= data_in; reg_ptr_b <= 4'd0; end
                     4'd6: begin wr6_b <= data_in; reg_ptr_b <= 4'd0; end
                     4'd7: begin wr7_b <= data_in; reg_ptr_b <= 4'd0; end
-                    //@4'd9: begin wr9_a <= data_in; reg_ptr_b <= 4'd0; end   // shared master-int-ctrl
+                    4'd9: begin wr9_a <= data_in; reg_ptr_b <= 4'd0; end     // shared master-int-ctrl
                     4'd10: begin wr10_b <= data_in; reg_ptr_b <= 4'd0; end
                     4'd11: begin wr11_b <= data_in; reg_ptr_b <= 4'd0; end
                     4'd12: begin wr12_b <= data_in; reg_ptr_b <= 4'd0; end
